1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,880
Alright, the last section of this chapter deals with lower urinary tract carcinoma.

2
00:00:05,300 --> 00:00:11,780
So the lower urinary tract includes the renal pelvis, the ureter, the bladder, and the urethra.

3
00:00:12,360 --> 00:00:16,180
And there are three types of cancers that classically arise from this location.

4
00:00:16,640 --> 00:00:22,600
The first is urothelial carcinoma, the second is squamous cell carcinoma, and the third is adenocarcinoma.

5
00:00:23,080 --> 00:00:25,620
Now as far as urothelial carcinoma is concerned,

6
00:00:25,620 --> 00:00:31,820
this is a malignant tumor that arises from the urothelial lining of the lower urinary tract.

7
00:00:31,980 --> 00:00:34,720
So the pelvis, the ureter, the bladder, or the urethra.

8
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:40,300
Now remember that the normal lining of the entire lower urinary tract is actually urothelial.

9
00:00:40,660 --> 00:00:45,360
And so therefore, this is the most common type of lower urinary tract cancer.

10
00:00:45,820 --> 00:00:49,480
The most common location within the lower tract is going to be the bladder.

11
00:00:49,920 --> 00:00:54,400
The number one risk factor for urothelial carcinoma is cigarette smoke.

12
00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:59,240
In particular, within cigarette smoke, polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons.

13
00:00:59,940 --> 00:01:02,340
Additionally, naphthalamine is a risk factor.

14
00:01:02,820 --> 00:01:06,580
Naphthalamine actually is one of the carcinogens present in cigarette smoke.

15
00:01:06,940 --> 00:01:11,860
And so therefore, it's just a further reminder that cigarette smoke is a major risk factor.

16
00:01:12,440 --> 00:01:14,100
Azo dyes are an important risk factor.

17
00:01:14,220 --> 00:01:15,220
These are used for coloring.

18
00:01:15,940 --> 00:01:22,140
For example, there was a study which showed that there was an increased incidence of urothelial carcinoma

19
00:01:22,140 --> 00:01:24,000
in hairdressers in Los Angeles.

20
00:01:24,520 --> 00:01:28,260
And of course, hairdressers were using dyes to color the hair of their clients.

21
00:01:28,860 --> 00:01:32,080
And so azo dyes increases the risk as well.

22
00:01:32,640 --> 00:01:37,880
And then finally, long-term cyclophosphamide can increase the risk for urothelial carcinoma,

23
00:01:38,220 --> 00:01:39,560
as can finacetin.

24
00:01:40,040 --> 00:01:44,020
And so these are two important high-yield associations to be aware of as well.

25
00:01:44,020 --> 00:01:47,880
Urothelial carcinoma is generally seen in older adults.

26
00:01:48,320 --> 00:01:50,940
The classic presentation is painless hematuria.

27
00:01:51,300 --> 00:01:54,560
And it's important to note that it arises via two distinct pathways.

28
00:01:55,060 --> 00:01:56,880
And this is particularly high-yield.

29
00:01:57,420 --> 00:02:01,940
In the papillary pathway, tumors develop as papillary growths.

30
00:02:01,940 --> 00:02:06,460
And remember that a papillary growth is essentially a fibrovascular core

31
00:02:06,460 --> 00:02:10,600
with a blood vessel running through it upon which epithelium is placed.

32
00:02:10,600 --> 00:02:16,100
And so this would be the papillary pattern of urothelial carcinoma.

33
00:02:16,500 --> 00:02:19,900
Now, important to note that in the papillary pathway,

34
00:02:20,320 --> 00:02:24,480
the tumors start as low-grade and then progress to high-grade,

35
00:02:25,160 --> 00:02:27,200
and then eventually they invade.

36
00:02:28,260 --> 00:02:31,380
And so this is the classic progression of the papillary pathway.

37
00:02:31,740 --> 00:02:33,760
The second pathway is called the flat pathway.

38
00:02:34,140 --> 00:02:38,340
And in this particular pathway, you develop high-grade urothelial carcinoma

39
00:02:38,340 --> 00:02:40,760
that develops as a flat lesion.

40
00:02:41,160 --> 00:02:43,600
Now, important to note that this tumor does not start low-grade,

41
00:02:43,720 --> 00:02:47,180
but starts as a high-grade tumor that eventually progresses to invade.

42
00:02:47,780 --> 00:02:51,340
So again, the papillary pathway begins as a low-grade tumor

43
00:02:51,340 --> 00:02:53,500
that progresses to high-grade and then invades.

44
00:02:53,780 --> 00:02:57,320
And the flat pathway begins as high-grade and then eventually invades.

45
00:02:57,700 --> 00:02:59,800
Another important thing to note about the flat pathway

46
00:02:59,800 --> 00:03:03,640
is that it is associated with early P53 mutations,

47
00:03:04,140 --> 00:03:06,320
and that is particularly high-yield.

48
00:03:06,440 --> 00:03:10,540
Again, early P53 mutations in the flat pathway.

49
00:03:11,180 --> 00:03:12,820
Here's an image of the flat pathway,

50
00:03:13,080 --> 00:03:15,580
and you can clearly see here's a lamina propria.

51
00:03:15,760 --> 00:03:17,640
This epithelium is sitting on a basement membrane,

52
00:03:17,640 --> 00:03:21,740
and that this lesion appears flat but high-grade.

53
00:03:22,460 --> 00:03:24,340
And here's a picture of the papillary pathway

54
00:03:24,340 --> 00:03:27,300
where you can clearly see that you've got these blood vessels

55
00:03:27,300 --> 00:03:31,240
with fibrous tissues sitting in the middle of these finger-like projections.

56
00:03:31,420 --> 00:03:33,620
Here's a classic picture of a finger-like projection.

57
00:03:34,220 --> 00:03:36,480
And again, this papillary pathway begins as low-grade,

58
00:03:36,640 --> 00:03:38,140
then eventually progresses to high-grade,

59
00:03:38,460 --> 00:03:39,840
and then eventually invades.

60
00:03:40,460 --> 00:03:42,600
Now, patients who develop urothelial carcinoma

61
00:03:42,600 --> 00:03:46,200
often have multifocal tumors and often show recurrence.

62
00:03:46,720 --> 00:03:49,380
And the reason is that the entire urothelial surface

63
00:03:49,380 --> 00:03:53,080
is actually mutated in a phenomenon known as the field defect.

64
00:03:53,640 --> 00:03:56,560
Now, here the idea is that the entire urothelium

65
00:03:56,560 --> 00:03:59,460
has been hit with carcinogens over a long period of time,

66
00:03:59,640 --> 00:04:01,200
especially in a person who smokes.

67
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:05,160
And so the entire field, all of the urothelial surface,

68
00:04:05,460 --> 00:04:06,640
is essentially mutated.

69
00:04:06,640 --> 00:04:09,580
And it's only a matter of time before multiple tumors develop.

70
00:04:10,060 --> 00:04:11,860
And so patients often have multiple tumors,

71
00:04:12,020 --> 00:04:13,340
and they often have recurrence

72
00:04:13,340 --> 00:04:14,880
because there are so many mutations

73
00:04:14,880 --> 00:04:17,160
lining the surface of that urothelium.

74
00:04:17,540 --> 00:04:19,080
Again, that's called a field defect.

75
00:04:19,620 --> 00:04:21,200
The second type of tumor that can arise

76
00:04:21,200 --> 00:04:24,000
from the lower urinary tract is squamous cell carcinoma.

77
00:04:24,340 --> 00:04:26,920
Of course, this is a malignant proliferation of squamous cells,

78
00:04:27,260 --> 00:04:28,680
and it usually involves the bladder.

79
00:04:29,180 --> 00:04:31,020
Now, remember that the lower urinary tract

80
00:04:31,020 --> 00:04:34,060
does not normally have any squamous epithelium.

81
00:04:34,460 --> 00:04:36,820
And therefore, in order to form a squamous cell carcinoma,

82
00:04:37,120 --> 00:04:39,280
you must first develop squamous metoplasia.

83
00:04:39,600 --> 00:04:42,320
Of course, that metoplasia would eventually become dysplasia,

84
00:04:42,620 --> 00:04:45,400
and the dysplasia would eventually become squamous cell carcinoma.

85
00:04:45,400 --> 00:04:49,160
The key risk factors for squamous cell carcinoma are chronic cystitis.

86
00:04:49,620 --> 00:04:52,960
Chronic inflammation can result in squamous metoplasia,

87
00:04:53,240 --> 00:04:55,160
which can then eventually progress to cancer.

88
00:04:55,820 --> 00:04:59,960
One very high-yield risk factor is schistosoma hematobium infection.

89
00:05:00,440 --> 00:05:02,220
This is classically seen in the Middle East,

90
00:05:02,500 --> 00:05:05,720
and on board exams, it's classically seen in a Middle Eastern male,

91
00:05:06,100 --> 00:05:08,240
particularly a young Middle Eastern male.

92
00:05:08,640 --> 00:05:10,320
And so what happens in this particular case

93
00:05:10,320 --> 00:05:14,580
is that the schistosoma hematobium embeds itself within the bladder wall.

94
00:05:14,960 --> 00:05:16,500
That results in chronic inflammation,

95
00:05:16,940 --> 00:05:19,780
and again, that chronic inflammation results in a squamous metoplasia,

96
00:05:20,040 --> 00:05:22,700
which then eventually progresses to squamous cell carcinoma.

97
00:05:23,120 --> 00:05:26,120
Another risk factor would be long-standing nephrolithiasis.

98
00:05:26,460 --> 00:05:27,700
And again, the same idea here

99
00:05:27,700 --> 00:05:30,140
is that with a chronic irritation of the urothelium,

100
00:05:30,220 --> 00:05:32,220
you develop a squamous metoplasia,

101
00:05:32,500 --> 00:05:34,960
and the squamous metoplasia eventually becomes cancer.

102
00:05:35,560 --> 00:05:38,200
The third type of lower urinary tract carcinoma

103
00:05:38,200 --> 00:05:39,660
is adenocarcinoma.

104
00:05:39,660 --> 00:05:42,540
This is, of course, a malignant proliferation of glands,

105
00:05:42,860 --> 00:05:44,360
and it usually involves the bladder.

106
00:05:44,820 --> 00:05:45,740
Now, it's important to note

107
00:05:45,740 --> 00:05:49,120
that you normally don't have glandular epithelium lining the bladder,

108
00:05:49,500 --> 00:05:52,700
and so therefore, this is only going to arise in particular circumstances.

109
00:05:53,240 --> 00:05:55,100
One super high-yield circumstance

110
00:05:55,100 --> 00:05:58,100
is that this can arise from a urachal remnant.

111
00:05:58,540 --> 00:06:01,600
Now, this is high-yield because it ties back into embryology.

112
00:06:02,260 --> 00:06:05,260
So let's just remind ourselves that the urachis is a duct

113
00:06:05,260 --> 00:06:07,540
that connects the fetal bladder with the yolk sac,

114
00:06:07,540 --> 00:06:11,040
and, of course, it allows the bladder to drain waste into the yolk sac.

115
00:06:11,440 --> 00:06:13,620
Now, normally, this would run from the dome of the bladder

116
00:06:13,620 --> 00:06:14,980
through the umbilical cord,

117
00:06:15,320 --> 00:06:18,120
and it is lined by glandular cells.

118
00:06:18,780 --> 00:06:22,520
If there's failure to involute the urachis such that there's a remnant,

119
00:06:22,800 --> 00:06:25,640
this remnant can actually then become adenocarcinoma.

120
00:06:25,640 --> 00:06:28,480
And it's important to note, for board examination purposes,

121
00:06:28,860 --> 00:06:32,580
that this adenocarcinoma would be present at the dome of the bladder.

122
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:36,420
Again, high-yield adenocarcinoma that arises from a urachal remnant

123
00:06:36,420 --> 00:06:39,040
would be present at the dome of the bladder.

124
00:06:39,460 --> 00:06:42,100
Another risk factor for the development of adenocarcinoma

125
00:06:42,100 --> 00:06:43,820
would be cystitis glandularis.

126
00:06:44,120 --> 00:06:46,640
This is where a patient has chronic inflammation of the bladder

127
00:06:46,640 --> 00:06:50,920
and gets a columnar metaplasia in response to that chronic inflammation.

128
00:06:51,520 --> 00:06:54,580
Of course, that columnar metaplasia could then become adenocarcinoma.

129
00:06:54,580 --> 00:06:59,020
Now, the final association with adenocarcinoma is bladder extrophy.

130
00:06:59,480 --> 00:07:03,280
In bladder extrophy, you get a congenital failure to form the caudal

131
00:07:03,280 --> 00:07:07,480
or the lower portion of the anterior abdominal and bladder walls.

132
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:11,240
And when this occurs, this exposes the surface of the bladder

133
00:07:11,240 --> 00:07:14,800
to the outside world, which then increases the risk in the future

134
00:07:14,800 --> 00:07:17,140
of development of an adenocarcinoma.

135
00:07:17,720 --> 00:07:21,060
And that concludes both the section as well as the chapter.

